# 120 - a chain of ors stops at the first operand that decides, evaluated left
# to right (§6 .33), so a() runs and neither b() nor c() does. Holds under
# either associativity, which is deliberate: this case is about
# short-circuiting, not about grouping (see 134 for grouping).
--- source
fn a() { print "a"; return true; }
fn b() { print "b"; return true; }
fn c() { print "c"; return true; }
print a() or b() or c();
--- expect
a
true
